// case: list-mixed-element-kinds
// what: a list has one element type, and pushing a second kind into it is a
//      type error rather than something to discover later. Before the element
//      type existed there was nowhere to put this. `var mixed = []` `mixed.push(1)`
//      `mixed.push("two")` stored an int and a string in the same 8-byte slot,
//      in whatever order they arrived, and every read of it was a `*(void**)`.
//      Nothing said so at the push and nothing could say so at the read, because
//      the read had no type to check the value against. That is the mechanism
//      behind F-0009, where `getOr(nums, 0, "d")` on an int list dereferenced
//      an int as a `char*` and the process died with SIGSEGV.
//
//      This case was a tests/suite program pinning the RUNTIME refusal:
//
//        $ orbit run tests/suite/list_mixed_element_kinds.orb
//        orbit: list element type mismatch
//          list holds int, this value is string
//          a list has one element type, so a read and a write must agree on it
//
//      The checker refuses it first now, so it cannot run at all, and the suite
//      runner asserts that every program in tests/suite builds. The refusal
//      moved up a stage; the record moved with it.
//
//      A heterogeneous list is not expressible in this shape of list. The
//      alternative is a boxed element -- a tagged union per slot -- which is a
//      much larger runtime change and still would not make `getOr(l, 0, "d")`
//      legal, because the read would have no declared type to check against either.
// expect-error: This list holds int, so what is pushed into it must be int
fn main() -> int {
    var mixed = []
    mixed.push(1)
    print("first push accepted")
    mixed.push("two")
    print("unreachable")
    return 0
}